EXPERIMENT 32: Dueling DQN for Gridworld Navigation
Aim

To use Dueling DQN to train an AI agent for optimal navigation in a complex Gridworld environment and compare its performance with standard DQN.

Algorithm / Procedure
Define the Gridworld states and possible actions.
Create a standard DQN neural network.
Create a Dueling DQN with separate value and advantage networks.
Train both networks using sample Gridworld data.
Predict Q-values for different states.
Select the action with the highest Q-value.
Compare the performance of DQN and Dueling DQN.
Program

Delete the previous Experiment 32 code completely and run this code from the beginning.

import numpy as np
from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense


# Number of states and actions
states = 16
actions = 4


# Sample Gridworld data
X = np.eye(states)


Y = np.random.rand(states, actions)


# -------------------------------
# Standard DQN
# -------------------------------


dqn = Sequential([
    Dense(32, activation="relu", input_shape=(states,)),
    Dense(32, activation="relu"),
    Dense(actions, activation="linear")
])


dqn.compile(
    optimizer="adam",
    loss="mse"
)


dqn.fit(X, Y, epochs=20, verbose=0)


dqn_values = dqn.predict(X, verbose=0)




# -------------------------------
# Dueling DQN
# -------------------------------


# Shared network
shared = Sequential([
    Dense(32, activation="relu", input_shape=(states,)),
    Dense(32, activation="relu")
])


# Value stream
value_model = Sequential([
    shared,
    Dense(1, activation="linear")
])


# Advantage stream
advantage_model = Sequential([
    shared,
    Dense(actions, activation="linear")
])


# Train both streams
value_model.compile(
    optimizer="adam",
    loss="mse"
)


advantage_model.compile(
    optimizer="adam",
    loss="mse"
)


value_model.fit(
    X,
    np.max(Y, axis=1),
    epochs=20,
    verbose=0
)


advantage_model.fit(
    X,
    Y,
    epochs=20,
    verbose=0
)


